WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.870
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:10.360
In this new age of AI, there is no shortage
of articles and discussion about AI safety,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:15.120
and of course, rightfully so: these new learning
algorithms started solving problems that were

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.110
previously thought to be impossible in quick
succession.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.600
Only ten years ago, if we told someone about
half of the things that have been covered

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.040
in the last few Two Minute Papers episodes,
we'd have been declared insane.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:32.680
And of course, having such powerful algorithms,
we have to make sure that they are used for

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:33.680
good.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.570
This work is a collaboration between OpenAI
and DeepMind's security team and is about

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.660
introducing more human control in reinforcement
learning problems.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.670
The goal was to learn to perform a backflip
through reinforcement learning.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.850
This is an algorithm that tries to perform
a series of actions to maximize a score.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.960
Kind of like playing computer games.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.760
For instance, in Atari Breakout - if we break
a lot of bricks, we get a high score so we

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.510
know we did something well.

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:05.289
If we see that happening, we keep doing what
led to this result, if not, we go back to

00:01:05.289 --> 00:01:07.470
the drawing board and try something new.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.390
But this work is about no ordinary reinforcement
learning algorithm, because the score to be

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:18.180
maximized comes from a human supervisor and
we're trying to teach a digital creature to

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.799
perform a backflip.

00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:24.729
I particularly like the choice of the backflip
here because we can tell when we see one,

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:29.729
but a mathematical specification of this in
terms of movement actions is rather challenging.

00:01:29.729 --> 00:01:35.340
This is a problem formulation in which humans
can overlook and control the learning process,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.060
which is going to be an increasingly important
aspect of learning algorithms in the future.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.319
The feedback option is very simple: we just
specify whether this sequence of motions achieved

00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:47.109
our prescribed goal or not.

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:50.450
Did it fall or did it perform the backflip
successfully.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:56.349
After around 700 human feedbacks, the algorithm
was able to learn the concept of a backflip,

00:01:56.349 --> 00:02:01.539
which is quite remarkable given that these
binary yes/no scores are extremely difficult

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:03.659
to use for any sort of learning.

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:08.479
In an earlier episode, we illustrated a similar
case with a careless teacher who refuses to

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:14.040
give out points for each problem on a written
exam and only announces whether we have failed

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.040
or passed.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.790
This clearly makes a dreadful learning experience,
and it is incredible that the algorithm is

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:21.790
still able to learn using these.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:27.489
We provide feedback on less than 1% of the
actions the algorithm makes, and it can still

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:32.079
learn difficult concepts off of these extremely
sparse and vague rewards.

00:02:32.079 --> 00:02:35.650
Low-quality teaching leads to high-quality
learing.

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:36.650
How about that!?

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:41.319
This is significantly more complex than what
other techniques were able to learn with human

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:42.319
feedback.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:44.120
And, it works with other games too!

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:45.970
A word about the collaboration itself.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:51.160
When a company hires a bunch of super smart
scientists and a spends a ton of money on

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:55.810
research, it is understandable that they want
to get an edge through these projects, which

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:58.579
often means keeping the results for themselves.

00:02:58.579 --> 00:03:03.219
This leads to excessive secrecy and a lack
of collaboration with other groups as everyone

00:03:03.219 --> 00:03:05.900
wants to keep their cards close to their chest.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.980
The fact that such collaborations can happen
between these two AI research giants is a

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:16.480
testament to how devoted they are to working
together and sharing their findings with everyone,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.659
free of charge for the greater good.

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:19.659
Awesome.

00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:24.329
As the media is all up in arms about the demise
of the human race I feel that it is important

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:26.680
to show the other side of the coin as well.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.090
We have top people working on AI safety right
now.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:34.280
If you wish to help us tell these stories
to more people, please consider supporting

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.569
us on Patreon.

